any events occur in an interval of time (one hour).\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of time or space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07160410-a73d-5491-ac7b-6d22b8d2282e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419431} {:answer/id #uuid "207e7398-1d2d-564f-924d-9241209c572e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419439} {:answer/id #uuid "93d90b19-71ca-57e8-931e-35cadfebadbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419471} {:answer/id #uuid "bd12d504-3244-5d03-bea3-5eee58c7022d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419487} {:answer/id #uuid "fda6c9a1-29dd-579a-9d70-e1bef8c9abd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fda6c9a1-29dd-579a-9d70-e1bef8c9abd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419513} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc8a185-a6bc-582b-bc0a-ce5101e00aad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a68d38c1-253b-59e6-ad1e-93208422e4e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-131412" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The average points scored on a math test in a class\n- The number of orders a firm receives per month\n- The number of calls a firm will receive next week for help concerning a toy" :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. The average points scored on the math test is not an event that occurs at a constant average rate.\n\n- Random variable II follows a Poisson distribution. The number of orders can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a month).\n\n- Random variable III follows a Poisson distribution. Again, the number of calls can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a week).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e4b3024-3180-5c86-b5c4-b0932c98377b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419448} {:answer/id #uuid "82d023e2-5bed-57ab-93f9-0516b548003c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419465} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1e0977-7098-5e67-84ee-6897e0e3087c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419494} {:answer/id #uuid "ddaedfa0-ff2b-5bad-b874-0a1668ef36ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419500} {:answer/id #uuid "f866e90f-16bb-5bd1-8827-73b69f68f662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e4b3024-3180-5c86-b5c4-b0932c98377b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419448} :answer-field/id #uuid "eda912e0-867b-5e29-86d2-2c6727a332af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d411b05d-363a-5c38-b187-cabf795e2dff" :question/math-academy-id "q-85316" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled using a Poisson distribution?\n\n- The number of visits a web page has per minute\n- The number of works of art created by a sculptor in a period of one year\n- The average height of the players on a basketball team" :question/worked-solution "A Poisson distribution models the number of events that occur in an interval of time or space if events occur independently and at a constant average rate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I follows a Poisson distribution. The number of visits on a web page can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a minute).\n\n- Random variable II follows a Poisson distribution. The number of works of art can be modeled as independent events with a constant average rate, and we wish to know how many events occur in an interval of time (a year).\n\n- Random variable III does *not* follow a Poisson distribution. There are no events that occur at a constant average rate, and there is no interval of time or space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419633 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "209a85c7-16ad-5315-9321-6bb903fa5398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419605} {:answer/id #uuid "28bfae27-6981-5691-9a5a-62fdf2e3c47c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419606} {:answer/id #uuid "7596282f-3290-5e4c-b0bc-7c7fefc0d1f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419613} {:answer/id #uuid "aaaf34e3-c318-5109-9b20-14ae45f46079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419621} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d05e4d-4d94-5fc7-aaf2-511184e63ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28bfae27-6981-5691-9a5a-62fdf2e3c47c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419606} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f52fc05-4e89-5fdf-9a0b-799264a128a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "237ee44c-ddeb-50a9-b28f-87d4edfa4e60" :question/math-academy-id "q-212521" :question/problem "Given that $-21≡-12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=3$\n- $n=9$\n- $n=12$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=-21-(-12)=-9.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $3∣(-9)$ because $(-9)÷3=-3,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $9∣(-9)$ because $(-9)÷9=-1,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=9.$\n\n- Note that $12∤(-9).$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=12.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152075 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a07c5ec7-8199-53c7-9738-80e360d03b38" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Modulus"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e51f6df-e206-53ac-8c6a-e8c968fdbab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419609} {:answer/id #uuid "56156d58-5991-5469-83af-0516720e5fe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419611} {:answer/id #uuid "845c3642-d5b7-51cc-8c24-5c719388349e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419616} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd62da4-495d-5a88-9b81-83fe4d43949a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419623} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e785cd-3ae0-5934-b08f-1e3d607da075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "845c3642-d5b7-51cc-8c24-5c719388349e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419616} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ffa6280-410f-5863-a63a-238138cfb0ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7dad68af-5609-5774-82ac-907f4e4304f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-76962" :question/problem "Given that $19≡5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\n- $n=7$\n- $n=9$\n- $n=2$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=19-5=14.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $7∣14$ because $14÷7=2,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=7.$\n\n- Note that $9∤14.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=9.$\n\n- Note that $2∣14$ because $14÷2=7,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51ed1169-4dcb-5651-bbbd-c7b884f63478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419610} {:answer/id #uuid "5eef336e-d43f-594c-8c6c-e0250235815a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419612} {:answer/id #uuid "85e9b13d-72c1-546a-b6fc-eafc300cdb59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419617} {:answer/id #uuid "965cecae-b949-5417-b3cc-5e430c2f2b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419619} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f886d7-3b46-5e40-b8ea-797128add774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85e9b13d-72c1-546a-b6fc-eafc300cdb59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419617} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b9776a6-3e68-58ea-b6e5-5ee338447367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c37fbba7-25b2-5279-9899-188d8dedca8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-103380" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-7≡27\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$\n- $-5≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $11≡18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $5∤(-7-27).$ Notice that $-7-27=-34,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Statement II is false because $6∤(-5-15).$ Notice that $-5-15=-20,$ which is not divisible by $6.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $7\\,|\\,(11-18).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-18)÷7=-7÷7=-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bc1a27e-8427-57e6-ac2e-b947a4b1556a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419603} {:answer/id #uuid "7a197d0d-9913-5fb2-bf3c-4835cbe300af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419614} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9d3ee8-5a6d-521f-b277-71c7303fe530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419615} {:answer/id #uuid "8a94c9aa-c1e4-5350-85e8-39c118369fb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419618} {:answer/id #uuid "c959a564-e622-5a0d-8e5b-fc810aa60b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bc1a27e-8427-57e6-ac2e-b947a4b1556a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419603} :answer-field/id #uuid "3da5eb72-bc77-5ebc-a47a-3006549126c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e9350880-d00e-5d41-adb3-52fc6c8e0846" :question/math-academy-id "q-113664" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-5≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$\n- $-23≡-5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)$\n- $-7≡-1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $7∤(-5-8).$ Notice that $-5-8=-13,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement II is true because $6∣(-23-(-5)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-23-(-5))÷6=-18÷6=-3\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true because $2\\,|\\,(-7-(-1)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-7-(-1))÷2=-6÷2=-3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152074 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2eebe007-8a95-578d-b553-e37a7bd8a327" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Negative Integers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14749e55-fcd9-5cab-b71b-ab77bde17dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419604} {:answer/id #uuid "31c492b8-531d-5421-96e8-37acf0421d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419607} {:answer/id #uuid "436c60b7-f2a7-5cc5-ada7-157322f4a843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419608} {:answer/id #uuid "98edc3ec-d794-589b-8439-5ef103206375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419620} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6cf5ba-4043-5780-8f58-4c07333d4125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98edc3ec-d794-589b-8439-5ef103206375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419620} :answer-field/id #uuid "7adb391f-ba37-5aef-9e23-79d9c1428c63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ee3460a5-a9ce-5793-8247-ccd3db242273" :question/math-academy-id "q-113675" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-36≡-19\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$\n- $15≡-26\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$\n- $-27≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $7∤(-36-(-19)).$ Notice that $-36-(-19)=-17,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement II is false because $10∤(15-(-26)).$ Notice that $15-(-26)=41,$ which is not divisible by $10.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $4\\,|\\,(-27-9).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-27-9)÷4=-36÷4=-9\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12910645-377d-54ef-bc0b-422296caf8f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419661} {:answer/id #uuid "2090546c-8891-5b10-af98-9204905ac08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419662} {:answer/id #uuid "27f9e9c7-23c3-5ee6-82bd-1ed602f60cb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419664} {:answer/id #uuid "39e6d5c4-ef80-5978-98b7-b10c7c9e811a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419665} {:answer/id #uuid "da77cae2-0a6d-576c-9bbf-669849034a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2090546c-8891-5b10-af98-9204905ac08a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419662} :answer-field/id #uuid "14b5fd09-d567-55da-9493-1ac629b8d62a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e737a421-d643-51f0-9d67-b9556fcc15c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-97060" :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $B$ to basis $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "559e653a-2ab9-54a8-84e3-02bf106a8f47" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d5b6db-f2d7-57e8-a2a5-1d92b46e0512" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420031} {:answer/id #uuid "60887104-df4a-53c7-a034-7138745e6c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420036} {:answer/id #uuid "75e38757-7df1-5cf4-b8a8-25b31d2f0338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420038} {:answer/id #uuid "afaa078f-f8ba-5d53-96fa-429e2e0695f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420048} {:answer/id #uuid "e34f1333-ff13-539f-98a3-6487bacbdcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e34f1333-ff13-539f-98a3-6487bacbdcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420056} :answer-field/id #uuid "96a743b7-fda2-51fe-bfdd-1a87d90bbfe6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "48fbd1ea-16f2-572a-98a1-c1983af51b24" :question/math-academy-id "q-221923" :question/problem "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $3∣(14x+21y)$\n- $7∣(14x+21y)$\n- $4∣(14x+21y)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $14(1)+21(0)=14,$ which is not divisible by $3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n14x+21y=7(2x+3y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $2x+3y$ must be an integer, too. So, $7∣(14x+21y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $14(1)+21(0)=14,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "559e653a-2ab9-54a8-84e3-02bf106a8f47" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da65dc0-01b4-5b68-9fb5-2b24392ab3e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420032} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0c5418-7d81-5d69-80db-f47bba1fe715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420040} {:answer/id #uuid "81fb3c0b-8063-5f9d-b742-0b572de22bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420042} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5ebb79-57bf-59e0-84ab-0b8b6661babd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420044} {:answer/id #uuid "cfef5e04-f733-5d5c-8a6b-71ad9b16f171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfef5e04-f733-5d5c-8a6b-71ad9b16f171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420054} :answer-field/id #uuid "39c81008-53e2-57d5-9021-9e6bc5fda77a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63936aa1-e722-5014-a82a-6fe76f4b851d" :question/math-academy-id "q-221929" :question/problem "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $2$ divides $(6x-18y)$\n- $3$ divides $(6x-18y)$\n- $5$ divides $(6x-18y)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n6x-18y=2(3x-9y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $3x-9y$ must be an integer, too. So, $2$ divides $(6x-18y).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n6x-18y=3(2x-6y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $2x-6y$ must be an integer, too. So, $3$ divides $(6x-18y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=2$ and $y=1,$ we have $6(2)-18(1)=-6,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092223 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b86558a-4c99-5ccb-a100-719692a056f4" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 90 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10cf90fb-e29e-510d-8cf4-93075f7dd6be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420099} {:answer/id #uuid "7699eaf3-de8c-5053-898a-a4c8beee968f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420100} {:answer/id #uuid "99a9553b-c748-5118-a8d9-ca0c58ba4325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420101} {:answer/id #uuid "e9ef7977-1930-5dd3-9b84-4d0b1b641d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420102} {:answer/id #uuid "eadf43e5-1274-5818-a95a-76c46e2e08da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99a9553b-c748-5118-a8d9-ca0c58ba4325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420101} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fc3ccce-1ebd-5f22-9b8f-6e3842468c4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "08bf8696-2d24-58a9-b5b4-856286859054" :question/math-academy-id "q-160646" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-1,3),(-2,2),(1,-4).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(3,1)$\n- $(2,-2)$\n- $(-4,-1)$" :question/worked-solution "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${90}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of ${270}^{∘}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{90}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(3,1),(2,2),(-4,-1).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb430d5-5241-56a2-96be-73f44b0f2114" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Transitivity of Divisibility"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37d1ed1f-2a27-5962-9f76-7e68504f6b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420198} {:answer/id #uuid "92f86242-b5e9-5e13-99b0-1f387aeb933c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420220} {:answer/id #uuid "a873a377-31d6-5d14-bc3d-af2b01b12bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420223} {:answer/id #uuid "d6e70fa3-3d87-548c-a566-669bf0527f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420232} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c3abe4-7795-5469-8fa0-9b120fc335e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37d1ed1f-2a27-5962-9f76-7e68504f6b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420198} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4feefa3-f467-563c-b592-67d4467afe30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24815c66-50ab-5aab-ac7b-25ddc673e132" :question/math-academy-id "q-221381" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $11∣k$ then $33∣k$\n\nII. If $33∣k$ then $11∣k$\n\nIII. If $33∣k$ then $3∣k$" :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ we have transitivity of divisibility:\n\n$a∣b\\,$ and $\\,b∣c$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $a∣c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let $k=11.$ Then, $11∣11$ but $33∤11.$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $11∣33.$ Therefore, we have\n$11∣33$ and $33∣k,$ which implies that $11∣k.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $3∣33.$ Therefore, we have\n$3∣33$ and $33∣k,$ which implies that $3∣k.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b3101aa-ee9e-5940-b170-2f4e78802150" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Divisibility of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25938baa-529b-5f86-828c-2801671fe4d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420192} {:answer/id #uuid "3cabb55e-40d7-5b81-a1ac-1144923f9b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420200} {:answer/id #uuid "550ce662-95ae-5c42-94f7-d6898e204e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420205} {:answer/id #uuid "7760ef9f-a800-540a-87c6-3414de29afe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420216} {:answer/id #uuid "df951e81-7965-5468-a3e1-c106088a53a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df951e81-7965-5468-a3e1-c106088a53a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420238} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfb4bbf3-86cf-55ec-89a8-e2c06957f446" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42a248b1-4b2a-5a7f-b673-589ac28da9a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-93649" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $10\\,|\\,21b$ then $10\\,|\\,b$\n\nII. If $21\\,|\\,25b$ then $21\\,|\\,b$\n\nIII. If $15\\,|\\,9b$ then $15\\,|\\,b$" :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(10,21)=1.$ Therefore, we have $10\\,|\\,21b$ and $\\text{gcd}(10,21)=1,$ which implies $10\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(21,25)=1.$ Therefore, we have $21\\,|\\,25b$ and $\\text{gcd}(21,25)=1,$ which implies $21\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $b=5.$ Then, $15∣9\\cdot 5$ but $15∤5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b3101aa-ee9e-5940-b170-2f4e78802150" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Divisibility of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c38ff4-4134-5851-a52f-d46fca4ccc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420186} {:answer/id #uuid "142f0a78-ef1b-5c40-b87c-2e4e6e998e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420190} {:answer/id #uuid "28a1ea30-a1c0-5800-8caf-4ea2604323dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420193} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd1e956-1cac-5094-a64b-65c5d077b526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420207} {:answer/id #uuid "afdca230-1900-5d05-8b30-94bc667faa97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06c38ff4-4134-5851-a52f-d46fca4ccc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420186} :answer-field/id #uuid "25f31e32-b105-56ba-945b-18f1eba552b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "553ed68f-8e74-5dda-a8b3-cdd3e89c31cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-93650" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If ${19}^{2}\\,|\\,19b$ then ${19}^{2}\\,|\\,b$\n\nII. If $12\\,|\\,35b$ then $12\\,|\\,b$\n\nIII. If $22\\,|\\,{7}^{2}b$ then $22\\,|\\,b$" :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let $b=19.$ Then, ${19}^{2}∣19\\cdot 19$ but ${19}^{2}∤19.$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(12,35)=1.$ Therefore, we have $12\\,|\\,35b$ and $\\text{gcd}(12,35)=1,$ which implies $12\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(22,{7}^{2})=1.$ Therefore, we have $22\\,|\\,{7}^{2}b$ and $\\text{gcd}(22,{7}^{2})=1,$ which implies $22\\,|\\,b.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420262 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c37bf66e-a574-5cf1-9c1a-fdfda98ca27b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Divisibility by a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "144822ca-c64b-5d44-988e-8d3f58310385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420191} {:answer/id #uuid "6b662c83-9382-549d-b78f-d6ae45b36298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420214} {:answer/id #uuid "ae35d862-ca5e-5f85-a3fd-da77890fb2a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420225} {:answer/id #uuid "afc58b19-6e0c-5559-867b-c0befae68830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420226} {:answer/id #uuid "da7300a2-932d-5ccd-b80e-d390c021dccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da7300a2-932d-5ccd-b80e-d390c021dccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420234} :answer-field/id #uuid "f237d89a-f9be-5d52-a1df-add8fdc50be1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ddd359d-678b-53c8-9991-000fcf922944" :question/math-academy-id "q-93657" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $3\\,|\\,a$ and $2\\,|\\,a$ then $6\\,|\\,a$\n\nII. If $7\\,|\\,b$ and $14\\,|\\,b$ then $98\\,|\\,b$\n\nIII. If ${2}^{3}\\,|\\,c$ and $3\\,|\\,c$ then $24\\,|\\,c$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$a\\,|\\,c,$ $\\,b\\,|\\,c,\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $ab\\,|\\,c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(2,3)=1$ and $2\\cdot 3=6.$ Therefore, we have $(2\\,|\\,a),(3\\,|\\,a),$ and $\\text{gcd}(2,3)=1,$ which implies $(6\\,|\\,a).$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let $b=14.$ Then, $(7∣14)$ and $(14∣14),$ but $(98∤14).$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}({2}^{3},3)=1$ and ${2}^{3}\\cdot 3=24.$ Therefore, we have $({2}^{3}\\,|\\,c),(3\\,|\\,c),$ and $\\text{gcd}({2}^{3},3)=1,$ which implies $(24\\,|\\,c).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420264 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198311 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c37bf66e-a574-5cf1-9c1a-fdfda98ca27b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Divisibility by a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cbae4e4-2423-5869-9afa-da82e1b33b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420188} {:answer/id #uuid "63d2cb0f-4fa8-5c73-8c7f-8dfd3f5884fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420211} {:answer/id #uuid "9f28d17f-a614-54b1-8f45-4295cb2a9f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420221} {:answer/id #uuid "c915309d-8abd-5446-a1f9-f241c7dc3116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420228} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1d996a-1c8d-5142-913e-181d70bfe766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cbae4e4-2423-5869-9afa-da82e1b33b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420188} :answer-field/id #uuid "9800a386-864d-5b89-9fa0-d8050a82fd2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d7153a0-178f-57d1-b332-d3ea8e076028" :question/math-academy-id "q-93658" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $4\\,|\\,a$ and $9\\,|\\,a$ then $36\\,|\\,a$\n\nII. If $5\\,|\\,b$ and $7\\,|\\,b$ then $35\\,|\\,b$\n\nIII. If ${6}^{2}\\,|\\,c$ and $2\\,|\\,c$ then $72\\,|\\,c$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$a\\,|\\,c,$ $\\,b\\,|\\,c,\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $ab\\,|\\,c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(4,9)=1$ and $4\\cdot 9=36.$ Therefore, we have $(4\\,|\\,a),(9\\,|\\,a),$ and $\\text{gcd}(4,9)=1,$ which implies $(36\\,|\\,a).$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(5,7)=1$ and $5\\cdot 7=35.$ Therefore, we have $(5\\,|\\,b),(7\\,|\\,b),$ and $\\text{gcd}(5,7)=1,$ which implies $(35\\,|\\,b).$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $c=36.$ Then, $({6}^{2}∣36)$ and $(2∣36),$ but $(72∤36).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb430d5-5241-56a2-96be-73f44b0f2114" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Transitivity of Divisibility"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e1582c6-f975-57a2-bb84-29de70274193" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420196} {:answer/id #uuid "5d34d488-7731-54e1-adee-10ba7466acca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420208} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4b7a2b-3e52-5afd-8b75-c581575d9429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420213} {:answer/id #uuid "86a84e34-cc31-5936-aa5b-9bcfb65473f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420218} {:answer/id #uuid "a39f6118-e6af-5c4f-b439-8c10db5598d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b4b7a2b-3e52-5afd-8b75-c581575d9429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420213} :answer-field/id #uuid "185758a4-16ad-57b8-bf67-872651eda925" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "73c135de-ad71-58aa-a780-c01d04341240" :question/math-academy-id "q-221379" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $12∣h$ then $4∣h$\n\nII. If $4∣h$ then $2∣h$\n\nIII. If $3∣h$ then $12∣h$" :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ we have transitivity of divisibility:\n\n$a∣b\\,$ and $\\,b∣c$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $a∣c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $4∣12.$ Therefore, we have\n$4∣12$ and $12∣h,$ which implies that $4∣h.$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $2∣4.$ Therefore, we have\n$2∣4$ and $4∣h,$ which implies that $2∣h.$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $h=3.$ Then, $3∣3$ but $12∤3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b3101aa-ee9e-5940-b170-2f4e78802150" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Divisibility of a Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47123b5a-4d9e-5980-b7e8-1b9b31e1fa9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420201} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1afd65-b437-5472-b418-2dc1d6ebe865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420219} {:answer/id #uuid "a964c9b7-bccb-5c9b-b3c7-48de44c00f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I,II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420224} {:answer/id #uuid "dcb9bd8f-1205-50c0-a72f-14df58d7f13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420235} {:answer/id #uuid "e26579ec-ded1-5c35-9155-e69045a20a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcb9bd8f-1205-50c0-a72f-14df58d7f13a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420235} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5e3230f-cb4f-51e8-a721-e24f62b0c3ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420252}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f3b9fb7f-2262-5e34-bb97-3a915acfa755" :question/math-academy-id "q-93648" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,10b$ then ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,b$\n\nII. If ${17}^{2}\\,|\\,17b$ then ${17}^{2}\\,|\\,b$\n\nIII. If $20\\,|\\,{3}^{3}b$ then $20\\,|\\,b$" :question/worked-solution "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}({3}^{2},10)=1.$ Therefore, we have ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,10b$ and $\\text{gcd}({3}^{2},10)=1,$ which implies ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let $b=17.$ Then, ${17}^{2}∣17\\cdot 17$ but ${17}^{2}∤17.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(20,{3}^{3})=1.$ Therefore, we have $20\\,|\\,{3}^{3}b$ and $\\text{gcd}(20,{3}^{3})=1,$ which implies $20\\,|\\,b.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b642c0e-8b84-5e4a-891d-2e1beb9ad37e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420270} {:answer/id #uuid "593420ae-cc21-52d8-a8ed-d92dcd3ab3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420271} {:answer/id #uuid "b08a5b5d-77ac-5e98-91fc-de49ca37b422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420272} {:answer/id #uuid "fda4827e-23cb-5c38-a966-819a735816cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420273} {:answer/id #uuid "fdee9ac7-df88-5be7-8e6b-0c2c8b6fd156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fda4827e-23cb-5c38-a966-819a735816cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420273} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed30b9dd-3daf-5001-88c4-b9152d2bad1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba992cb2-fd2f-5e4c-b95b-b82e7bb29b58" :question/math-academy-id "q-232536" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)={3}^{x}+1?$\n\nI. The range of $f$ is $(1,\\infty )$\n\nII. $∀\\,y\\in (1,\\infty ),$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\nIII. $f$ maps $R$ **into** $R$" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/0c/0cb3e23cecdbd82803f5757827f21a424672bdde6da03782ca995d23e1c4d7b4.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the function takes all $y$-values greater than $y=1$, excluding $y=1$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $(1,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $(1,\\infty )$, then for all $y$ contained within $(1,\\infty ),$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=0\\in R.$ So, $f$ maps $R$ *into* $R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Trigonometric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c6dea98-f9b5-5483-ad34-13ce6f559a2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420310} {:answer/id #uuid "486b0493-eb45-55ed-82f5-c032db770e60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420312} {:answer/id #uuid "a0d2d1d1-0842-520f-b8fa-9b61ab89e6fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420318} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f92ab3-81aa-5ff4-8365-5163fc68e5f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420323} {:answer/id #uuid "faa7110f-20ad-5b9f-af55-e84b316f4f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "faa7110f-20ad-5b9f-af55-e84b316f4f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420324} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ef252b3-0c31-53e5-9944-1acf64287b65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "787ecee8-f1f0-5525-9f42-a25a7295c8af" :question/math-academy-id "q-181669" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\pi )=0}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2q\\pi )=0}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\pi )=0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x\\pi )=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{m}{2},\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{m}{2}\\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nB={…,-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{3}{4},…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420330 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ca3f93-b276-5422-a4f0-a2e4f89be083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420308} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb5e67c-cb84-5412-9a3e-61b4099aeabd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420309} {:answer/id #uuid "65e1ba83-f0a5-5cc9-8f4a-3ea942a47e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420313} {:answer/id #uuid "6907e3ce-e2f4-5289-974d-b7b687315b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420315} {:answer/id #uuid "a0223240-2eb5-5bc2-b389-218324c528fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6907e3ce-e2f4-5289-974d-b7b687315b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420315} :answer-field/id #uuid "981d986a-6dfa-540d-944a-7be3c24f9b94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a6b24cf2-2976-579e-8d0e-dd268f3c65a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-181641" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{1-2x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nII. $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(3{e}^{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nIII. $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(4{e}^{-x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{1-2x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{1-2x})=-2{e}^{1-2x} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{1-2x})=4{e}^{1-2x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{1-2x})=-8{e}^{1-2x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={{e}^{1-2x},-2{e}^{1-2x},4{e}^{1-2x},-8{e}^{1-2x},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =3{e}^{x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x})=3{e}^{x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x})=3{e}^{x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{x})=3{e}^{x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nB={3{e}^{x}}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =4{e}^{-x} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-x})=-4{e}^{-x} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4{e}^{-x})=4{e}^{-x} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-x})=-4{e}^{-x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains only two elements:\n\n$$\nC={4{e}^{-x},-4{e}^{-x}}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C|=2,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420331 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198270 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Trigonometric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "099f3a38-4da4-5649-a22d-eba7684b0cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420305} {:answer/id #uuid "1137ea51-0849-5c10-9f4b-2be034d8e893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420306} {:answer/id #uuid "43cc4526-e59c-5a0d-b9a8-f705fb0bf7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420311} {:answer/id #uuid "c84a5364-9750-5067-9737-5e4daa30aa9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420319} {:answer/id #uuid "dde382cc-266f-5db0-88f1-85b4627ded80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43cc4526-e59c-5a0d-b9a8-f705fb0bf7a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420311} :answer-field/id #uuid "21e37987-951e-54e4-a566-e39dd06571da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8147626-87d7-56ca-be09-d1e1490d794e" :question/math-academy-id "q-181667" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2})=0}$\n\nII. $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{q\\pi }{2})=0}$\n\nIII. $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x\\pi }{2} & =m\\pi  \\\\ x & =2m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-2,0,2,4,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Since $A$ is infinite, and $A⊆B,$ it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198271 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1488dfbe-7dda-5765-aaae-fecd9ca9caa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420307} {:answer/id #uuid "671c41b3-d8d4-5559-b179-e68f0f12a414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420314} {:answer/id #uuid "872b5930-b555-5b00-977e-8ecaeb8ec451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420316} {:answer/id #uuid "c9a4b8b0-70fb-5140-bd89-b7cb08f1ab9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420320} {:answer/id #uuid "d21b5f4e-d145-5b7f-b843-065f3a5904e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420321}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1488dfbe-7dda-5765-aaae-fecd9ca9caa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420307} :answer-field/id #uuid "5beabf59-4d47-5db3-877d-e192dae60847" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d8b98aed-4075-5dac-9091-d486170b806a" :question/math-academy-id "q-181706" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nII. $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\sqrt{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n\nIII. $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}})=-\\frac{6}{25\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}},\\frac{2}{5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}},-\\frac{6}{25\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{8}}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is also infinite. Similar to the previous case, listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =-\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\sqrt{x})=-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})=\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}} \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={-\\sqrt{x},-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also infinite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})=\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420353 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19fc08ca-72fe-570b-a146-ccf5baf503d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420333} {:answer/id #uuid "2c504700-553a-587d-937f-efdc724865fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420335} {:answer/id #uuid "3d56a853-a4e2-5868-a11b-a8fda7f31398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420336} {:answer/id #uuid "462e1c30-a350-5b46-a5e5-cd8202fb5f1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420337} {:answer/id #uuid "7c57cc06-ff92-5adf-9092-769447c60c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c504700-553a-587d-937f-efdc724865fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420335} :answer-field/id #uuid "3924ef22-234b-5f76-ad06-7ddc5f55eff3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2486be8-75b8-51f1-882e-08f8a79961f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-42463" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n\n- $A$ has identical rows\n- $A$ has proportional columns\n- $A$ has a zero row" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical rows.\n\n- $A$ does *not* have proportional columns.\n\n- $A$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4b47a31-7bda-5366-bac4-1d070776e776" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25029d74-6c31-52ad-8e40-faedc9e473db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420400} {:answer/id #uuid "4d14ebfe-075c-5ad1-ac77-3e3c8fa226f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420401} {:answer/id #uuid "618e807a-28cb-599f-9e9f-ea6004064c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420402} {:answer/id #uuid "b23e5cb8-be50-53e9-bf24-a7374c9dcd80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420403} {:answer/id #uuid "f2516513-8240-538a-acb7-6adbea086ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d14ebfe-075c-5ad1-ac77-3e3c8fa226f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420401} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed0ab4b8-5966-5883-ba7e-805d59df8d8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5414df0-8556-5960-86b0-386e378b451e" :question/math-academy-id "q-213229" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9cddd6584fee52757b1c7a470a6eeffee3678d569e36025ca70c99e5543c71c2.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent a relation, defined on the set $A={\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\delta },$ that is neither reflexive nor irreflexive?" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nSince $R$ must be neither reflective nor irreflexive, some of the points $(\\alpha ,\\alpha ),$ $(\\beta ,\\beta ),$ and $(\\delta ,\\delta )$ must be in $R,$ and others must not.\n\nThese ordered pairs correspond to the diagonal points on the plots, as shown below:\n\n![](images/7d/7d4257c9e249f9a4c2a53c5566292e21ae6dfa7f0e08e27042548da2d8e7e000.png)\n\n \n\nA plot corresponding to a relation that is neither reflexive nor irreflexive must contain some diagonal points, but not all of them. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "007def61-c0db-5afc-857c-bb1794f44c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420498} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac9e6ca-aa28-5456-80e0-6b2c6eed18c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420499} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4787e2-6983-5165-b71f-60ddbc84d9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420500} {:answer/id #uuid "89c37704-a55e-55ef-8110-12970ee2c9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420501} {:answer/id #uuid "e9eece08-2814-52b9-9cbf-85f258fc3528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c4787e2-6983-5165-b71f-60ddbc84d9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420500} :answer-field/id #uuid "236e449f-8bdf-5cb7-898e-9834bb6e7cf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420503}] :question/id #uuid "d1a7556c-36af-5f0b-b64c-a00089e6850e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37456" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(1,1)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0$\n\nIII. The point $(1,1)$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=2\\cdot 0-{1}^{2}=-1<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=2>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)<0,$ then $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198269 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3" :knowledge-point/title "Sets Defined Using Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a84f9d9-81b1-54ae-8ae5-8c05ca06f33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420580} {:answer/id #uuid "7a2e9409-26c5-5b50-9445-3a7e0cc1dff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420581} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0ac365-22bb-5a1d-8ee4-097642b3a96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A,B,$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420582} {:answer/id #uuid "dd52b8a6-5559-526c-9e66-cb99de5e9630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420583} {:answer/id #uuid "f63bb298-46ab-531c-9644-243a96de9d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f63bb298-46ab-531c-9644-243a96de9d51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420584} :answer-field/id #uuid "0012b7e2-7b21-52ce-a8e6-93abfde08a7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7a7b8c64-0e50-5904-90c5-c8b0f74761de" :question/math-academy-id "q-181771" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\nI. $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,-{n}^{2}+n+12\\le 0}$\n\nII. $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,-{m}^{2}+m+12\\ge 0}$\n\nIII. $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,-{q}^{2}+q+12\\ge 0}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+x+12$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+x+12 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-x-12) & =0 \\\\ -(x-4)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=4,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/57/57d58b579557ba0ea8c6179bd37209c8199eee83e17d79c74828a051aa1382d6.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+x+12\\le 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx\\le -3\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 4\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [4,\\infty ).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+x+12\\ge 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n-3\\le x\\le 4\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=[-3,4].$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{n}^{2}+n+12\\le 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={…,-4,-3}\\cup {4,5,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{m}^{2}+m+12\\ge 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=8,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $-{q}^{2}+q+12\\ge 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420689} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420690} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420695} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420910} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420912} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592